# PISKA 33 — 6:6
srow(33, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND THESE WORDS WHICH I COMMAND YOU THIS DAY SHALL BE UPON YOUR HEART" (6:6) — RABBI: WHY IS IT SAID? BECAUSE IT IS SAID "AND YOU SHALL LOVE THE LORD YOUR GOD WITH ALL YOUR HEART" (6:5) — I DO NOT KNOW HOW ONE LOVES THE OMNIPRESENT; THE TEACHING SAYS "THESE WORDS … UPON YOUR HEART": PUT THESE WORDS ON YOUR HEART, AND SO YOU COME TO KNOW HIM WHO SPOKE AND THE WORLD WAS AND CLEAVE TO HIS WAYS. The head row of piska 33 (on 6:6; {SP_(33, 1, "רבי", "אומר", "למה", "נאמר")} "Rabbi says: why is it said"): 6:6 read as the HOW of 6:5 — the love a procedure whose first step is the words on the heart (the design\'s "four duties" open here); the row cites 6:6 and 6:5 in both files (the one head row citing two verses — the ink\'s head list, computed). The ink: "these words" eight seats in the book; "upon your heart" 6:6 and Ezekiel 38:10 alone (computed). The middah: a command read as the means of its neighbor (I12 — from the context; E28 from the preceding). Fresh in both files.')
srow(33, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'"WHICH I COMMAND YOU THIS DAY" — THAT THEY BE NOT IN YOUR EYES AS AN OLD EDICT THAT NO ONE HEEDS, BUT AS A NEW EDICT THAT ALL RUN TO READ. ({SP_(33, 2, "כדיוטגמא", "ישנה")} "as an old edict" against {SP_(33, 2, "כדיוטגמא", "חדשה")} "as a new edict"): "this day" read as EVERY day\'s newness — the Greek loan-word for the imperial edict the figure. The ink: "which I command you this day" eighteen seats in the book (computed); the chapter\'s "I command you" at 6:2 and 6:6 alone (computed). The middah: "this day" read beyond its letter (the scope moves\' form, E12-E25 — a word said of one day applying to every day). Fresh in both files.')
srow(33, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"UPON YOUR HEART" — FROM HERE R. JOSIAH SAID: A MAN MUST ADJURE HIS INCLINATION, FOR EVERYWHERE YOU FIND THE RIGHTEOUS ADJURING THEIR INCLINATION. ({SP_(33, 3, "צריך", "אדם", "להשביע", "את", "יצרו")} "a man must adjure his inclination"): the heart of 6:6 the seat of the inclination (32:3\'s two), governed by an OATH — the row\'s proofs the next row\'s four oaths; the Sifrei on Numbers 88 the parallel (the English\'s note). The middah: a rule drawn from the righteous\' practice (I3\'s kin — the father built from 33:4\'s cases). Fresh in both files.')

# PISKA 34 — 6:7
srow(34, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL TEACH THEM DILIGENTLY TO YOUR SONS" (6:7) — THAT THEY BE SHARP IN YOUR MOUTH: WHEN A MAN ASKS YOU A THING, DO NOT STAMMER BUT ANSWER AT ONCE — "SAY TO WISDOM, YOU ARE MY SISTER" (PROVERBS 7:4), "BIND THEM ON YOUR FINGERS" (7:3), "YOUR ARROWS ARE SHARP" (PSALM 45:6 — "PEOPLES FALL UNDER YOU"), "AS ARROWS IN A WARRIOR\'S HAND, SO ARE THE SONS OF YOUTH" (127:4-5). The head row of piska 34 (on 6:7; {SP_(34, 1, "שיהו", "מחדדים", "בתוך", "פיך")} "that they be sharpened in your mouth"): ושננתם ("and you shall teach them diligently") read from its ROOT — to sharpen, to whet — the proofs all arrows; THE INK AGREES: the root\'s nine seats are the whetted arrow, the sword, the tongue (Deuteronomy 32:41, Isaiah 5:28, Psalms 45:6, 64:4, 73:21, 120:4, 140:4, Proverbs 25:18) and 6:7 THE ONE SEAT of the word in this form (computed on the lemma 8150); Kiddushin 30a\'s seat ("sharpened" — the docket\'s); the store\'s gloss "and-point-them" the same root, rewritten at run 3. The middah: the verb read by its root\'s other seats (E7\'s kin — the shared word gathering its instances; a REFERENCE the Sifrei draws by the root, LR1). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'ANOTHER WORD: "AND YOU SHALL TEACH THEM DILIGENTLY" — THESE ARE IN THE RECITATION, BUT "SANCTIFY TO ME EVERY FIRSTBORN" (EXODUS 13:2) AND "AND IT SHALL BE WHEN HE BRINGS YOU" (13:11) ARE NOT IN THE RECITATION; FOR ONE MIGHT ARGUE: IF "SPEAK … FRINGES" (NUMBERS 15:37-38), WHICH IS NOT IN THE BINDING, IS IN THE RECITATION, THEN THE TWO EXODUS PASSAGES, WHICH ARE IN THE BINDING, SHOULD BE IN THE RECITATION — THE TEACHING SAYS "THESE": THESE ARE RECITED, THOSE ARE NOT. THE SHEMA\'S TEXT DEFINED ({SP_(34, 2, "אלו", "בשנון")} "these are in the recitation"): the RECITATION (6:4-9, 11:13-21, Numbers 15:37-41) and the BINDING (Exodus 13:1-10, 13:11-16, 6:4-9, 11:13-21) two sets with two members each way — the fringes recited, not bound; the Exodus passages bound, not recited; the a-fortiori that would merge them refused by "these". The ink: the four passages of the binding are the four seats of "for a sign upon your hand" — Exodus 13:9, 13:16, 6:8, 11:18, in four spellings of "your hand" (computed); "when your son asks you tomorrow" 6:20 and Exodus 13:14 the same two portions (computed). The middot: I1 stated and refused by a restrictive "these" (E2\'s kin); the case row Mishnah Berakhot 2:2 (the docket\'s). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'STILL I MIGHT SAY: IF "AND THE LORD SAID" (NUMBERS 15:37), PRECEDED BY OTHER COMMANDMENTS, IS RECITED, THE TEN WORDS, PRECEDED BY NONE, SHOULD BE RECITED — OR THE REVERSE FROM THE EXODUS PASSAGES; THE TEACHING SAYS "AND YOU SHALL TEACH THEM": THESE ARE RECITED, THE TEN WORDS ARE NOT. THE TEN WORDS OUT OF THE RECITATION ({SP_(34, 3, "עשר", "הדברות")} "the ten words"): the second candidate refused — the Decalogue, though "preceded by no commandments", not in the daily reading; Berakhot 12a\'s seat (the ten words once read with the Shema in the Temple, then stopped — the design\'s docket); chapter 5\'s reading answered by the shelf: the two copies of the ten words are not the creed. The middah: two a-fortiori arguments set against each other and both cut by the ink\'s "these" (I1 twice, refused). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'"TO YOUR SONS" — THESE ARE YOUR DISCIPLES; EVERYWHERE DISCIPLES ARE CALLED SONS: "THE SONS OF THE PROPHETS AT BETHEL" (2 KINGS 2:3), AT JERICHO (2:5) — NOT THEIR OFFSPRING BUT THEIR DISCIPLES. ({SP_(34, 4, "אלו", "תלמידיך")} "these are your disciples"): "your sons" widened to the taught — the teacher\'s duty, not the father\'s alone; the ink: "to your sons" at 6:7 and "your son and your son\'s son" at 6:2 — "your son\'s son" 6:2 and Exodus 10:2 (computed). The middah: a word\'s sense fixed from its other seats (E7\'s kin — "sons" at 2 Kings the exemplar; LR1: the Sifrei\'s own). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 5, 'CONTEXT', f'SO HEZEKIAH TAUGHT ALL THE TORAH TO ISRAEL AND CALLED THEM SONS ("MY SONS, BE NOT NEGLIGENT", 2 CHRONICLES 29:11); AND AS DISCIPLES ARE SONS, THE MASTER IS FATHER ("MY FATHER, MY FATHER", 2 KINGS 2:12, 13:14). ({SP_(34, 5, "וקראם", "בנים")} "and he called them sons"). Fresh.')
srow(34, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL SPEAK OF THEM" — MAKE THEM THE MAIN THING AND NOT SECONDARY: YOUR DEALINGS ONLY IN THEM; DO NOT MIX OTHER THINGS WITH THEM LIKE SO-AND-SO — LEST YOU SAY, I HAVE LEARNED ISRAEL\'S WISDOM, I WILL GO LEARN THE NATIONS\': "TO WALK IN THEM" (LEVITICUS 18:4), NOT TO BE FREE OF THEM; "THEY SHALL BE YOURS ALONE" (PROVERBS 5:17). ({SP_(34, 6, "עשם", "עקר", "ואל", "תעשם", "טפלה")} "make them the main thing and not secondary"): "speak OF THEM" read as "of them ONLY" — the restriction (E2\'s kin); the ink: "and you shall speak of them" one seat (computed); So-and-So Elisha ben Avuyah (the English\'s note). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 7, 'CONTEXT', f'"WHEN YOU WALK IT SHALL LEAD YOU; WHEN YOU LIE DOWN IT SHALL KEEP YOU; WHEN YOU AWAKE IT SHALL TALK WITH YOU" (PROVERBS 6:22) — THIS WORLD, THE HOUR OF DEATH, THE DAYS OF THE MESSIAH, THE WORLD TO COME. ({SP_(34, 7, "בהתהלכך", "תנחה", "אותך")} "when you walk it shall lead you" — the proverb\'s three postures the verse\'s "walk … lie down … rise"). Fresh.')
srow(34, 8, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND WHEN YOU LIE DOWN" — MIGHT IT BE EVEN IF HE LAY DOWN AT MIDDAY? THE TEACHING SAYS "AND WHEN YOU RISE" — MIGHT IT BE EVEN IF HE ROSE AT MIDNIGHT? THE TEACHING SAYS "WHEN YOU SIT IN YOUR HOUSE AND WHEN YOU WALK BY THE WAY": THE TORAH SPEAKS OF THE WAY OF THE WORLD. THE TIMES OF THE RECITATION ({SP_(34, 8, "יכול", "אפלו", "שכב", "בחצי", "היום")} "might it be even if he lay down at midday"): the four clauses of 6:7 read as TWO TIMES — the hour men lie down (evening) and the hour men rise (morning) — not one man\'s act; Mishnah Berakhot 1:1-2 the case rows (from when is the Shema recited in the evening, in the morning — the docket\'s at 4b). The ink: "when you sit … when you rise" 6:7 and 11:19 alone (computed); the store\'s glosses of the clauses rewritten at run 3 ("when-you-sit", "when-you-lie-down", "when-you-rise"). The middah: two clauses that would each be read alone bounded by a third (I13\'s form — the two decided by the third; the "way of the world" the decider). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 9, 'MATERIAL', f'ONCE R. ISHMAEL WAS RECLINING AND EXPOUNDING AND R. ELAZAR BEN AZARIAH UPRIGHT; THE TIME OF THE SHEMA CAME — R. ISHMAEL STRAIGHTENED, R. ELAZAR RECLINED. "WHAT IS THIS, ELAZAR?" — "ISHMAEL MY BROTHER, THEY SAID TO ONE: WHY IS YOUR BEARD GROWN? — TO SPITE THE SHAVERS." — "YOU RECLINED BY THE HOUSE OF SHAMMAI, AND I STRAIGHTENED BY THE HOUSE OF HILLEL." THE DISPUTE ON THE POSTURE ({SP_(34, 9, "מטה", "ודורש")} "reclining and expounding"): the House of Shammai reads 6:7\'s "when you lie down … when you rise" as POSTURES (recline in the evening, stand in the morning); the House of Hillel as TIMES, each man in his way ("when you walk by the way" — 34:8\'s reading) — Mishnah Berakhot 1:3, Tosefta 1:4 (the English\'s note; the case row with its dispute flag, the docket\'s). The middah: one clause read two ways by two houses (the dispute row — I13\'s open form, no third verse). Fresh in both files.')
srow(34, 10, 'CONTEXT', f'ANOTHER WORD: THAT THE THING BE NOT FIXED AS A DUTY — FOR THE HOUSE OF SHAMMAI SAY: IN THE EVENING ALL RECLINE AND RECITE, IN THE MORNING THEY STAND. ({SP_(34, 10, "שלא", "יקבע", "הדבר", "חובה")} "that the thing be not fixed as a duty" — R. Ishmael\'s reason: not to let Shammai\'s posture become law). Fresh.')

# PISKA 35 — 6:8
srow(35, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL BIND THEM" (6:8) — THESE ARE IN THE BINDING, BUT "AND THE LORD SAID … FRINGES" (NUMBERS 15:37) IS NOT IN THE BINDING; FOR ONE MIGHT ARGUE: IF "SANCTIFY TO ME" AND "WHEN HE BRINGS YOU" (EXODUS 13:2, 13:11), NOT IN THE RECITATION, ARE IN THE BINDING, THEN THE FRINGES, IN THE RECITATION, SHOULD BE IN THE BINDING — THE TEACHING SAYS "AND YOU SHALL BIND THEM": THESE ARE BOUND, THAT IS NOT. The head row of piska 35 (on 6:8; {SP_(35, 1, "אלו", "בקשירה")} "these are in the binding"): 34:2\'s mirror — the tefillin\'s four passages defined from the binding\'s side; the fringes excluded. The ink: "and you shall bind them" at {U("וקשרתם")} (computed); "for a sign upon your hand" four seats (computed). The middot: I1 refused by "these" (as 34:2). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'STILL I MIGHT SAY: THE EXODUS PASSAGES, PRECEDED BY OTHER COMMANDMENTS, ARE BOUND — THEN THE TEN WORDS, PRECEDED BY NONE, SHOULD BE BOUND; OR THE REVERSE FROM THE FRINGES; THE TEACHING SAYS "AND YOU SHALL BIND THEM": THESE ARE BOUND, THE TEN WORDS ARE NOT. THE TEN WORDS OUT OF THE TEFILLIN ({SP_(35, 2, "עשר", "הדברות")} "the ten words"): 34:3\'s mirror; the tefillin hold the four passages and not the Decalogue (Menachot 34b\'s territory — the docket\'s). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL BIND THEM FOR A SIGN UPON YOUR HAND" — ONE ROLL OF FOUR PASSAGES; FOR ONE MIGHT REASON: SINCE THE TORAH SAID PUT TEFILLIN ON THE HAND AND ON THE HEAD, AS THE HEAD HAS FOUR COMPARTMENTS SO THE HAND — THE TEACHING SAYS "FOR A SIGN UPON YOUR HAND": ONE ROLL OF FOUR. ({SP_(35, 3, "כרך", "אחד", "של", "ארבע", "טוטפות")} "one roll of four passages"): the hand\'s tefillin ONE compartment holding four, the head\'s FOUR — "a sign" singular the ground; Menachot 34b (the docket\'s). The middah: the analogy from the head refused by the singular (E2\'s kin). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'OR AS THE HAND HAS ONE ROLL, SO THE HEAD? THE TEACHING SAYS "THEY SHALL BE FOR FRONTLETS" — טטפת ("frontlet"), טטפת ("frontlet"), טוטפת ("frontlets"): FOUR PASSAGES; OR FOUR CASES, ONE FOR EACH? THE TEACHING SAYS "FOR A MEMORIAL BETWEEN YOUR EYES" (EXODUS 13:9) — ONE CASE OF FOUR. THE COUNT FROM THE SPELLINGS ({SP_(35, 4, "טוטפת", "טוטפות", "הרי", "ארבע", "טוטפות")} "frontlet, frontlets — behold four frontlets"): the shelf counts the three seats of "frontlets" as 1 + 1 + 2 — 6:8 and 11:18 DEFECTIVE (one each), Exodus 13:16 PLENE (two) — four compartments; THE INK DIVERGES AT 11:18: the DB\'s three seats read לטטפת ("for frontlets", 6:8, defective), לטוטפת ("for frontlets", 11:18, WITH THE VAV), ולטוטפת ("and for frontlets", Exodus 13:16, with the vav) — computed and asserted (the ink\'s FRONT) — so the count as the DB spells it is not the shelf\'s (the Talmud\'s form Sanhedrin 4b, Menachot 34b, the same count; the English translator transliterates 11:18 "totaft" as the shelf reads it): A DIVERGENCE OF THE INK FROM THE SHELF ON A SPELLING — filed to the compile (4b) as the tefillin cell\'s open row, the Mekhilta Pisha 17 the parallel (the English\'s note). The middah: a spelling read as a count (E10\'s kin), then "memorial" singular for the one case. Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'"UPON YOUR HAND" — ON THE HEIGHT OF THE HAND; YOU SAY THE HEIGHT, OR "UPON YOUR HAND" AS IT SOUNDS? REASON DECIDES: SINCE THE TORAH SAID PUT TEFILLIN ON THE HAND AND ON THE HEAD, AS THE HEAD\'S IS ON THE HEIGHT OF THE HEAD SO THE HAND\'S ON THE HEIGHT OF THE HAND. ({SP_(35, 5, "בגובה", "שביד")} "on the height of the hand" — the upper arm): "hand" widened from the palm to the arm by analogy with the head (35:12); Menachot 37a (the docket\'s). The middah: the two limbs read by one rule (E8 binyan av\'s form). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ELIEZER: "UPON YOUR HAND" — THE HEIGHT; OR AS IT SOUNDS? THE TEACHING SAYS "FOR A SIGN FOR YOU" — A SIGN FOR YOU AND NOT FOR OTHERS. ({SP_(35, 6, "רבי", "אליעזר", "אומר")} "R. Eliezer says"): "a sign" read as private — the arm under the sleeve; the ink: "for a sign upon your hand" the four seats (computed). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 7, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ISAAC: THE HEIGHT OF THE HAND — THE TEACHING SAYS "AND THESE WORDS SHALL BE UPON YOUR HEART" (6:6): OPPOSITE THE HEART, THE HEIGHT OF THE HAND. ({SP_(35, 7, "רבי", "יצחק", "אומר")} "R. Isaac says"): 6:6\'s "upon your heart" made the PLACE of 6:8\'s binding — the arm opposite the heart; the chapter read as one paragraph (E28 from the preceding). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 8, 'MATERIAL', f'"UPON YOUR HAND" — THIS IS THE LEFT; YOU SAY THE LEFT, OR IS IT THE RIGHT? THOUGH THERE IS NO PROOF THERE IS A HINT: "MY HAND FOUNDED THE EARTH AND MY RIGHT HAND SPREAD THE HEAVENS" (ISAIAH 48:13), "HER HAND TO THE PEG AND HER RIGHT HAND TO THE HAMMER" (JUDGES 5:26) — "HAND" UNQUALIFIED IS THE LEFT. ({SP_(35, 8, "זה", "שמאל")} "this is the left"): the arm chosen by the word\'s use — "hand" beside "right hand" in two verses; Menachot 36b-37a (the docket\'s); "a hint, not a proof" — the shelf\'s own grading of its evidence. The middah: a word\'s sense fixed from seats where it stands beside its opposite (E7\'s kin; LR1 the Sifrei\'s own). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 9, 'MATERIAL', f'R. NATHAN: "AND YOU SHALL BIND THEM … AND YOU SHALL WRITE THEM" (6:8-9) — AS THE WRITING IS WITH THE RIGHT, SO THE BINDING WITH THE RIGHT. ({SP_(35, 9, "מה", "כתיבה", "בימין", "אף", "קשירה", "בימין")} "as writing is with the right, so binding is with the right"): the left arm proved from the binding HAND — the two verbs of 6:8 and 6:9 paired (the ink: both consecutive perfects with the plural suffix — "bind them", "write them"; the morphology). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 10, 'MATERIAL', f'R. YOSE THE NET-MAKER: WE FIND THE RIGHT ALSO CALLED "HAND" (GENESIS 48:17 — "HIS FATHER\'S HAND" OF THE RIGHT HAND); IF SO, WHY "UPON YOUR HAND"? TO INCLUDE THE ONE WITHOUT A LEFT ARM, WHO PUTS IT ON HIS RIGHT. ({SP_(35, 10, "שאף", "ימין", "קרויה", "יד")} "that the right too is called hand"): the counter-seat to 35:8 turned into a case (the amputee); Menachot 37a. Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 11, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL BIND THEM FOR A SIGN UPON YOUR HAND, AND THEY SHALL BE FOR FRONTLETS BETWEEN YOUR EYES" — SO LONG AS THE HAND\'S IS ON THE HAND, PUT THE HEAD\'S ON THE HEAD; FROM HERE THEY SAID: PUTTING ON, THE HAND FIRST THEN THE HEAD; TAKING OFF, THE HEAD FIRST THEN THE HAND. THE ORDER ({SP_(35, 11, "כל", "זמן", "שליד", "ביד", "תן", "שלראש", "בראש")} "so long as the hand\'s is on the hand, put the head\'s on the head"): the verse\'s ORDER of clauses (hand, then "and they shall be" on the head) made the order of the acts — Menachot 36a (the docket\'s). The middah: the sequence of the ink as its logic (E11\'s kin — the order read as a rule). Fresh in both files.')
srow(35, 12, 'MATERIAL', f'"BETWEEN YOUR EYES" — ON THE HEIGHT OF THE HEAD; OR AS IT SOUNDS? REASON DECIDES: AS THE HAND\'S IS ON THE HEIGHT OF THE HAND SO THE HEAD\'S ON THE HEIGHT OF THE HEAD; R. JUDAH: AS THE HEAD\'S PLACE IS ONE THAT CAN BE UNCLEAN WITH ONE PLAGUE-MARK, SO THE HAND\'S — A PLACE FIT FOR ONE MARK. The piska\'s close ({SP_(35, 12, "בגובה", "שבראש")} "on the height of the head"): "between your eyes" moved from the bridge of the nose to the hairline by the same analogy as 35:5, R. Judah\'s ground the plague-law\'s "place" (Leviticus 13) — the ink: "between your eyes" five seats, 14:1\'s the fifth (the mourners\' baldness "between your eyes" — the same phrase for the head\'s hair; computed). Menachot 37a-b. The middah: E8 (the two limbs by one rule); the second ground from another law\'s definition of a place (a TRANSFER the shelf teaches — LR1). Fresh in both files.')

# PISKA 36 — 6:9
srow(36, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL WRITE THEM" (6:9) — A PERFECT WRITING; FROM HERE THEY SAID: ONE WHO WROTE ALEFS AS AYINS OR AYINS AS ALEFS, BETS AS KAFS, GIMELS AS TSADIS, DALETS AS RESHES, HES AS HETS, VAVS AS YODS, ZAYINS AS NUNS, TETS AS PES, BENT AS STRAIGHT, MEMS AS SAMEKHS, CLOSED AS OPEN, A CLOSED PARAGRAPH AS OPEN OR OPEN AS CLOSED, NOT IN INK, THE SONG AS PROSE OR PROSE AS THE SONG, THE NAMES IN GOLD — IT IS HIDDEN AWAY. The head row of piska 36 (on 6:9; {SP_(36, 1, "כתב", "שלם")} "a whole writing"): וכתבתם ("and you shall write them") cut as כתב תם ("a perfect writing") — the scribe\'s law of letter shapes and paragraph gaps, the mezuzah and the scroll invalid by any confusion; Shabbat 103b the seat (the docket\'s). THE INK BESIDE IT: the letters the shelf pairs are confused nowhere in the machine\'s store — but the store DROPS 6:4\'s two LARGE letters (the ayin and the dalet — the ink\'s exact miss; RESEARCH_LOG 2026-09-09 and 2026-09-17): the shelf\'s "ayin for alef" rule and the scroll\'s enlarged ayin are one letter\'s two marks. The middah: a word divided (E30 notarikon). Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL WRITE THEM ON THE DOORPOSTS" — I MIGHT HEAR: ON STONES? YOU REASON: "WRITE" IS SAID HERE AND "WRITE" AT 27:8 ("ON THE STONES") — AS THERE ON STONE, SO HERE; OR: "WRITE" HERE AND "WRITE" AT NUMBERS 5:23 (THE SCROLL AND INK) — AS THERE ON A SCROLL WITH INK, SO HERE; I DERIVE A THING FOR THE GENERATIONS FROM A THING FOR THE GENERATIONS (THE SCROLL), NOT FROM A THING FOR ITS HOUR (THE STONES); THOUGH NO PROOF, A HINT: JEREMIAH 36:18 "I WROTE THEM ON THE BOOK WITH INK". THE VERBAL ANALOGY WITH TWO CANDIDATES ({SP_(36, 2, "שומע", "אני", "על", "גבי", "אבנים")} "I might hear: on stones"): "write" at 6:9 joined by the SAME WORD to 27:8 (stones) or to Numbers 5:23 (scroll and ink) — the Sifrei choosing between two analogies by a RULE OF CHOICE (the standing over the momentary), then grading its own proof "a hint" — THE SHELF\'S OWN EXHIBIT OF I2\'S RECEPTION RULE: the shared word generates two joins; the teacher picks. The ink: "and you shall write them" 6:9 and 11:20 alone (computed). The middah: I2 (gezerah shavah — the Sifrei\'s own, LR1: taught, not ours), with the selection rule the row states; Mishnah Sotah 2:4 the English\'s note. Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"ON THE DOORPOSTS" — I MIGHT HEAR TWO DOORPOSTS; THE TEACHING SAYS IN THE SECOND PLACE "DOORPOSTS" (11:20) — AN EXTENSION AFTER AN EXTENSION RESTRICTS: ONE DOORPOST, THE WORDS OF R. ISHMAEL. ({SP_(36, 3, "רבוי", "אחר", "רבוי", "למעט")} "an extension after an extension — to restrict"): the plural at 6:9 and the plural at 11:20 read together as ONE post — THE INK: the two seats differ by one letter, 6:9\'s מזוזת ("doorposts") defective, 11:20\'s מזוזות ("doorposts") plene (the diff computed: one replace) — the two spellings the shelf\'s two "extensions". The middah: E3 — extension after extension restricts (the numbered rule itself, in R. Ishmael\'s name). Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ISAAC: NO NEED — "AND THEY SHALL PUT IT ON THE TWO DOORPOSTS AND ON THE LINTEL" (EXODUS 12:7) BUILDS A FATHER: WHEREVER "DOORPOSTS" IS SAID IT IS ONE UNLESS SCRIPTURE SPECIFIES TWO. ({SP_(36, 4, "אין", "צריך")} "there is no need"): the Passover\'s "two doorposts" the counter-seat — where the ink wants two it says two; the ink: "doorposts" thirteen seats in the Bible, Exodus 12\'s among them (computed). The middah: I3 (binyan av — the father built from Exodus 12:7). Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'"YOUR HOUSE" — ON THE RIGHT AS YOU ENTER; YOU SAY THE RIGHT AS YOU ENTER, OR AS YOU LEAVE? THE TEACHING SAYS "ON THE DOORPOSTS OF YOUR HOUSE" — YOUR COMING IN. ({SP_(36, 5, "על", "ימין", "בכניסה")} "on the right at the entering"): ביתך ("your house") heard as ביאתך ("your coming") — the right post of one entering; the word read by its sound (as 32:7, 32:21). Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND ON YOUR GATES" — I MIGHT HEAR THE GATES OF HOUSES, COOPS, BARNS, STRAW-SHEDS, CATTLE-SHEDS, STOREHOUSES, WOOD-SHEDS, WINE-CELLARS, GRAIN-STORES AND OIL-STORES; THE TEACHING SAYS "YOUR HOUSE": AS YOUR HOUSE IS A DWELLING, SO YOUR GATES — OF A DWELLING. ({SP_(36, 6, "שומע", "אני", "שערי", "בתים")} "I might hear: the gates of houses"): "gates" bounded by "house" — the dwelling the class; Yoma 11a the seat (the docket\'s). The middah: I6\'s form (the particular "your house" bounding the general "your gates"). Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 7, 'MATERIAL', f'I MIGHT HEAR EVEN THE GATES OF BASILICAS, PUBLIC HALLS AND BATH-HOUSES? THE TEACHING SAYS "YOUR HOUSE" — AS YOUR HOUSE IS A PLACE OF HONOR AND A DWELLING, SO YOUR GATES. ({SP_(36, 7, "שערי", "בסילקאות")} "the gates of basilicas" — the Greek loan-words): the second exclusion, honor added to dwelling. Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 8, 'MATERIAL', f'I MIGHT HEAR EVEN THE GATES OF THE SANCTUARY? THE TEACHING SAYS "YOUR HOUSE" — AS YOUR HOUSE IS PROFANE, SO THE SANCTUARY\'S GATES ONLY WHERE PROFANE; FROM HERE THEY SAID: THE CHAMBERS OPENING ON THE HOLY ARE HOLY, ON THE PROFANE PROFANE. ({SP_(36, 8, "שערי", "מקדש")} "the gates of the sanctuary"): the third exclusion; Mishnah Maaser Sheni 3:8 the case row (the English\'s note) — the chambers\' status by their opening. Fresh in both files.')
srow(36, 9, 'MATERIAL', f'BELOVED ARE ISRAEL, WHOM SCRIPTURE SURROUNDED WITH COMMANDMENTS — TEFILLIN ON THEIR HEADS AND ON THEIR ARMS, A MEZUZAH ON THEIR DOORS, FRINGES ON THEIR GARMENTS; OF THEM DAVID SAID "SEVEN TIMES A DAY I PRAISE YOU" (PSALM 119:164); ENTERING THE BATH-HOUSE HE SAW HIMSELF NAKED AND SAID: WOE, I AM NAKED OF COMMANDMENTS — AND REMEMBERED THE CIRCUMCISION AND PRAISED ("ON THE EIGHTH", PSALM 12:1). ({SP_(36, 9, "חביבים", "ישראל", "שסבבם", "הכתוב", "במצוות")} "beloved are Israel, whom Scripture surrounded with commandments"): the chapter\'s three objects (6:8 hand and head, 6:9 the door) with the fringes (Numbers 15) counted to David\'s SEVEN — 2 + 1 + 4 (the English\'s brackets); Menachot 43b the seat (the docket\'s). The middah: a number in one verse counted from the objects of another (E29\'s kin — the count, not the letter-values). Fresh in both files.')

